{"id":"f5ba2b35cdf609716e0a84a4dcc050e92b1a23bcf4939193416cacce230d6e2b","lamport":1,"author":"scual.project@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-10-10T12:12:27.857525042Z","format_version":1,"kind":"set-field","payload":{"fields":{"description":"## What to build\n\nA ticket's change history and its closed time become something the lib answers, built from the Operation log (plus set and comment changes for tickets still in the hybrid format). `ti history` prints readable changes (what field changed, to what, by whom, when) instead of raw operation keys and JSON blobs. The TUI \"recently closed\" ordering and dashboard, and `ti stats`, use the real closed time instead of falling back to the created time.\n\n## Why\n\n`ti history` currently reads the git-meta store directly with the pre-ADR-0002 key layout. Verified: after `ti state in-progress` and `ti close`, it prints lines like `set ops:0000…:<sha> → {\"id\":…}`. The TUI closed-time lookup uses the same layout and, by code reading (not reproduced), never matches op-based tickets, so closed time silently falls back to created time. `ti stats` documents the same created-time proxy, so \"closed in the last 7 days\" really means \"created and closed in the last 7 days\".\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `ti history` for a ticket that was moved to in-progress then closed shows readable entries for each change, with no raw operation ids or JSON\n- [ ] A lib-level test (using the test store) closes a ticket and asserts both the history entries and the closed time\n- [ ] The binary no longer opens the git-meta database directly for history or closed time\n- [ ] `ti stats` \"closed in last 7 days\" counts by close time, with a test where a ticket created long ago is closed today\n- [ ] TUI \"recently closed\" ordering uses the close time\n- [ ] Tickets in the hybrid format (ops plus legacy keys) still produce a sensible history\n- [ ] `ti verify` and existing history tests still pass\n\n## Blocked by\n\nNone — can start immediately.\n\n## Notes\n\nReinforces ADR 0002. Deepening: one reader behind TicketStore gives locality for log projection and leverage across history, stats and the TUI."}}}